You just touched on one of my pet peeves..

Ours mainly sounded like stepping on a cat.  A very loud cat at that.
Xbiff on one person's machine could annoy everybody 3-4 offices up &
down the hall.

When I upgraded our machines to 3.2C (ie, they were down
anyhow), I opened the case and put some duct tape over the speaker.
This seems to be the only way of queting down the beeps to a
reasonable volume.

A better solution might be to plug the speaker directly into the
motherboard.  However, the inside of a 200 is a horrible mess (a new
$2.5k Dell P100 has a much cleaner layout inside the case than a new
$17k Alpha 250 4/266.  Anybody at Digital listening??).  I really
don't want to go rooting around looking for a the correct plug on the
motherboard.

What is that stupid sound card doing there in the first place on a
workstation aimed at the scientific and engineering market?  In the
future, please make things like this an option, not a manadory feature
that adds to the cost of the system and annoys the heck out of the
users.  And while you're at it, please bring back decent cases on low
end machines.  Cases like the 3000/300 series were nice.  The
3000/{400|600|700} cases were WONDERFUL.  The AS{200|250} case is
worse than the case on most cut-rate PC-clones.

Drew

