>1. I looked in news.answers for a FAQ for this group and did not find 
>one.  Is there a FAQ for this group someplace else?

It must have expired at your site - check rtfm.mit.edu.  The OSF FAQ isn't
nearly as nice as other unixes FAQs, however.

>2. Our old system seemed to have a far superior manual system. Why for 
>example is there no man pages for the /usr/bin/del command? (Or for any 
>up/download commands.)?

/usr/bin/del does not exist.  Under aix, del looks to be the same as 
"rm -fi" on any unix system.  My guess is that's it's an AIX command, written
into that flavor of Unix by an IBM guy who was used to typing "del" on his
other machines.

ftp is the universal unix upload/download program, there's a man page for it.

rz/sz (zmodem) is a 3rd party program, as is kermit.  Your sysadmin will have
to install those packages before you can use them (let alone get a man page for
it). 

>3. Our old system had a very extensive description of the system called 
>InfoExplorer accessible with the info command. Does OSF/1 have anything 
>like this?

bookreader is the comparable DEC product, only it's not nearly as nice as 
info explorer.

