In Article <44bsg6$59m@shred.stanford.edu>
bbense@shred.stanford.edu (Booker C. Bense) writes:

>In article <44a7tc$s2b@news.service.uci.edu>,
>Steven White <srw@hedrock.ps.uci.edu> wrote:
>>In article <449ml6$qfc@hermes.acs.unt.edu>, srt@zaphod.csci.unt.edu (Steve Tate) writes:
>>|> 
>>|> The only real problem I have is with the "beep" of the machine ---
>>|> it's not very consistent, except in the fact that it always sounds
>>|> really bad.  Sometimes it's as little as a light "click" sound, and
>>|> sometimes it sounds like I stepped on a cat or something.
>
>- - Well, I know what the problem is, but not how to fix it. The problem is one
>basic to all DSP chips. The software is blasting stuff at the chip before it's
>initialized. That's why you get that weird "clip" sound. Everybody in our group
>has the same problem. Just another example of DEC's great software quality. 

I believe this can be corrected by adjusting the bell volume. If you are not
running CDE it is in the "Keyboard..." window of the Session Manager "Options"
menu. I believe this will initialize the DSP and things will then work
reasonably.

Having said that, I can't confim it because I have a 3000/300LX. But I ws given
this fix by a Digital employee who had the problem,

