: management duties of several DEC Alphas, three of which are running
: Digital Unix v3.2.  One problem - the previous manager forgot to leave
: me the root password on the Unix machines... he's unavailable for two 
: weeks, and I've got to take care of some software installations before
: then;  any computer hijackers out there that can help me break in?
: Surely there's an accepted method... ?  I'm at a total loss, though, so
: any help is appreciated.  Responses by either email or post to this group
: are fine.. thanks a ton.

1. Boot to single-user.

   >>> boot -fl s

2. Either:

   2a. Make root writeable

       # mount -u /

       Check & mount /usr (if you want to use vi or some such)

       # /etc/fsck -p /dev/</usr device>
       # mount /usr

   Or:

   2b. Check & mount all the local filesystems (the lazy person's way. ;)

       # /sbin/bcheckrc

3. Change password

   # passwd
   New password: blah
   Verification: blah

4. Continue to multi-user mode

   # init 3

