Subject: Re: CAP on OSF/1 3.1 - compiling native Ethertalk
Date: 1995/09/29
Message-ID: <44g4e2$c78@news.service.uci.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 110981674
references: <42nj2j$ki4@hermes.cair.du.edu> <439adu$4r7@news.cc.oberlin.edu> <439hat$fnc@news.service.uci.edu> <44b2ku$pd3@lyra.csx.cam.ac.uk>
organization: University of California, Irvine
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

In article <44b2ku$pd3@lyra.csx.cam.ac.uk>,
Dave Hodgson  <dmh@sanger.ac.uk> wrote:
>Please, please would someone, who has sucessfully compiled CAP with native
>Ethertalk, post or (better), mail a list of the things they did to port it to
>OSF v3.x??

Get a copy of CAP v6 pl 192 or higher.  Unpack it.  Follow the directions
to configure it.  

If you are running OSF/1 2.0 or higher, you'll need to modify the the line
in the m4.setup file that looks like this:

define([etherpobjs],[spfiltp.o pfopen.o])

Remove pfopen.o from the line.

If you are running OSF/1 1.x, you'll need to copy /usr/examples/packfilter/pfopen.c
to support/ethertalk so it can be used during the compile of CAP.

Compile and install it.

Make sure your kernel has the packetfilter option turned on; if not,
turn it on, rebuild your kernel, and reboot.

Start cap.


