mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1
x-mailer: Mozilla 1.1 (X11; I; OSF1 V3.2 alpha)

Here's code that will find the amount of RAM on a Digital UNIX
system.    I had to cast the integer address to a char to silence
the compiler warning.  Although the man page for getsysinfo indicates
a integer pointer is expected for the argument, the compiler insists
on a character pointer.  I didn't really have the time to investigate the 
discrepancy so it stayed.  Perhaps you might want to look at it!

Compile with cc -std1   or cc -migrate (to get better error messages)


#include <stdio.h>
#include <errno.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/sysinfo.h>
#include <machine/hal_sysinfo.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
int     TotalMemory;


if (getsysinfo(GSI_PHYSMEM,(char *)&TotalMemory,(unsigned
                          long)(sizeof(int)),0,NULL) !=-1)
   {
   TotalMemory += 512;
   fprintf(stdout,"Total Memory = %d MB\n",TotalMemory/1024);
   exit(0);
   }
perror("PHYSMEM");
exit(1);
}

--Pete

