In article <NEWTNews.812073590.22831.jim@DIALUP01.wr.com.au>, jdnash@ozemail.com.au writes:

>Anybody have any information regarding the performance 
>of a RAID 5 configuration with various numbers of
>component disks?  We have some data which suggests
>that the varying the "chunk" size from the default
>does not generally result in huge gains (Oracle
>app, not TP intensive, very large database), but
>we have no info on how the # of disks in each
>group affects anything.

It depends on what the I/O looks like.  If the application
does serial small random I/Os, the number of members may
not change the performance much.  If you have many such
applications, then each disk added to the array offers
more potential requests that can be serviced.  Similarily,
if the application is multi-threaded and does many I/Os
in parallel, extra members may help (until the array becomes
bandwidth limited).

If the application is bandwidth limited, then it will depend
on the underlying configuration.  Six disks on a single bus
will probably provide no more bandwidth than two disks on a
single bus.  If you have six disks spread across three
busses, then it may provide higher bandwidth than three
disks on the same three busses.  How you order the members
may also have an affect.

>
>OSF 3.2
>Alpha 2100 
>about 40 GB of disk to spread the "best" way

It would be helpful to know which RAID controller you're using.
