X-Deja-AN: 110792383
references: <4478fc$pk4@news.iastate.edu> <4479bn$qc8@news.iastate.edu>
organization: Indiana University Astrophysics, Bloomington, IN
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.sys.dec,comp.unix.osf.misc

Big Bik Bin <zzuo@iastate.edu> wrote:
>I just received a response from a consultant here. It says that
>if the processes are in a state of resource waiting state, the only
>chance for me to get rid of it is to become a su and reboot it. But I
>can't so both, so I have to ask someone else for helps.
>
>
>These things happen to me a lot:
>
>zzuo      5145  0.0  0.0 1.84M   0K ??  IWN  23:40:22     0:00.25 /usr/bin/tcsh
>zzuo     22837  0.0  0.0 36.0M   0K ??  U N    Sep 15     0:48.47 <exiting>
>zzuo      5145  0.0  0.0 1.84M   0K ??  IWN  23:40:22     0:00.25 /usr/bin/tcsh
>zzuo      5144  0.0  0.0 1.77M   0K ??  IW   23:40:21     0:00.13 sh -c /usr/bi
>
>Is there any better solution that is more convienient to the others and me?

These processes often occur because you're not properly handling things like
SIGCLD when terminating child processes.  If that's the case, then killing the
parent process also gets rid of the "exiting" process.  Try killinf the shell
script, or logging out and back in.

	Alec


