A product called the Digital Porting Assistant should help you in your
port.  It analyzes source code and points out problems and potential
problems.  It also includes extensive on-line help.

Version 1 is available now for FREE.  I have attached a summary of this product
at the end of this message.  If you want to get a copy send mail to 
port-comments@zko.dec.com.

Karen Michaels
Digital Porting Assistant Project Leader
Digital Equipment Corportation
--------------------------
                The Digital Porting Assistant
                              
The Porting Assistant is a tool that helps application
developers port their code to Digital UNIX(TM).  It analyzes C
and C++ code and identifies code that may not compile or run
properly.  The Porting Assistant reduces the time and cost
of porting an application to Digital UNIX.  It provides
quick access to technical porting information.  The Porting
Assistant is available now for free.

Features:
The Porting Assistant provides an interactive environment in
which the user can locate places in the code to change,
understand why they need to make the changes and then make
the change.  The Porting Assistant also provides a global
replace capability which allows the users to perform a
replace operation over a set of files.

Using the Porting Assistant, the user can run a check and
then look at the diagnostic messages that the check
produces.  The Porting Assistant allows the user to navigate
though the diagnostic messages using their editor (vi, emacs
or a Motif editor).  From the editor the user can see both
the diagnostic and the source code that caused the
diagnostic.

Checking code:

The Porting Assistant runs the following checks over source
code:

- locates conditional code (#ifdef) that might also need
  a Digital UNIX branch
- locates includes (#include) of files that don't exist
  and reports locations where a file of the same name exists
- locates calls to library functions that don't match
  Digital UNIX definitions
- locates code with 32-bit dependencies
- locates calls to library functions with different
  semantics on Digital UNIX

Some applications that people port to Digital UNIX may
already run on multiple platforms.  If this is the case,
they may use conditional code that applies to one or more
platform.  The Porting Assistant can locate this conditional
code, so that the developer can determine whether or not the
code applies to Digital UNIX.

Different operating systems store different include files in
different directories.  The Porting Assistant locates
include files that are not found in the specified location.
If possible, it reports locations where the file does exist.
For example, the file dkio.h is located in /usr/include/sun
on SunOS systems, but it is located in /usr/include/sys on
Digital UNIX systems.  The Porting Assistant will flag
references to /usr/include/sun/dkio.h and suggest that the
user include /usr/include/sys/dkio.h instead.

The Porting Assistant checks for functions that do not have
function definitions.  This may be the case if an
application calls a system function that is available on
another platform, but does not exist on Digital UNIX.  The
Porting Assistant also flags functions that have calls that
are inconsistent with their definition.  This may occur if a
function has a different signature on Digital UNIX, than on
another platform.

The Porting Assistant locates code that makes assumptions
that it is running on a 32-bit architecture.  This includes
operations such as assigning or casting pointers to
integers, assignments of longs to other types and sign
extension problems.

The Porting Assistant provides a list of functions that have
different semantics on Digital UNIX than on other platforms.
These functions are different because Digital UNIX is a 64-
bit architecture.  For each function, the Porting Assistant
provides access to the function's man page as well as a
special man page that describes how this function is
different on Digital UNIX.  The Porting Assistant searches
the user's code to find calls to these functions.

Help using the Porting Assistant and fixing problems:
The Porting Assistant includes online hypertext help that
describes how to use it and the relevance of each of its
checks.  Included in the online help are porting tips that
cover topics such as byte ordering, data alignment and
shared libraries.  In addition, the Porting Assistant
provides extensive help text for the errors that it reports.
This help text includes a description of the problem and a
suggestion on how the user can fix it.

Global Replace:
As part of porting, developers may have to change function
names, variable name or type name in their source files.
The Porting Assistant supports this activity through its
global replace capability.  It allows the user to change a
specified string to a different string in a set of files.
The Porting Assistant can perform the substitution for the
user with or without confirmation.  When the user wants to
confirm each substitution, the Porting Assistant brings each
file into the user's editor and positions on each occurrence
of the specified string.  The user can then indicate whether
or not they want to change the current occurrence of the
string.

Requirements:
The Porting Assistant requires a typical software
development configuration.  This consists of an Alpha
machine with the following configuration:

- 64 MB of memory
  
- DEC OSF/1 Alpha Operating System V3.0 or greater
  
- DEC OSF/1 Alpha UNIX C Developers' Extensions V3.0 or 
  greater

- 48 MB of disk space




