In article <42vr15$h86@dscomsa.desy.de> 
wbr@axher7.desy.de (Walter Brueckner) writes:
   The 21164 rates (in the Cern catalogue) at 88 Cern units.
   We measured the speed of two large applications, one
   monte carlo ("hmc") and one particle track reconstruction ("trident"),
   and the speed results are rather disappointing.
   Compared to the DEC 5000/260 (rated at 15 cern units) the speed
   factor should be almost 6, we measure only 4.5.
   Compared to the SGI R4400 at 200 MHZ (rated at 27 cern units) the
   speed factor should be around 3, we measure only 2.2 !

Well to be honest with you it doesn't surprise me at all that these
two programs don't share the same performance ratio as these 'cern
units' you're talking about! I'm sure I could bring a 21164 to its
knees with the right worst-case code...

When you compare individual SPECfp benchmarks, you get similarly large
variations in speedup / slowdown versus other processors.

The way I see it, the fact that the 21164 comes in at 88 cern units
means that it runs _that_ benchmark and _that_ benchmark alone, say, 8
times faster than a 11-cern-unit machine, and makes no guarantees
whatsoever about anything else being 8 times faster.

