In article <42q4fn$r8o@geraldo.cc.utexas.edu> jah@mojo.ots.utexas.edu (Jeff Hayward) writes:
>We have a 3000/700 running DU 3.2 which handles a lot of incoming
>SMTP connections.  At times, when there's a high rate of incoming
>connections (say, just after MCInet comes back from a long outage)
>we see connection attempts 'stall' in a half-open state.
>
>I'm assuming that this is because the TCP accept queue in the kernel
>is being overflowed by the rate of incoming SYNs.
>
>Has anyone else seen this problem, and is there some way I can give
>the kernel more resources to handle our load?

There's a kernel global, somaxconn, that controls the queue limit.
I think the default value is "8".  You should set it to be much
larger.  I would imagine that setting it to 1000 wouldn't hurt,
unless you have almost no RAM on the system.  I think this has
to be done using dbx, but there might be another way.

This will have no effect, however, unless you ALSO change the
second argument of the listen() system call (done in your SMTP
listener) to be equally large, or larger.  I'd try something
huge, since the kernel does min(backlog, somaxconn) and so whatever
you do here can't hurt.

No guarantees, of course.  This is not an official statement by
Digital Equipment Corp.

-Jeff



