Dxbook is the reader for the online documentation.  As I recall, 
a printed set of documentation is more than $1,000.  Dxbook is
supposed to let you view all the on-line documentation via X-terms
or at the console, and is also supposed to let you print any
portions you like.

Unfortunately, dxbook crashes about once every fifteen minutes of use.
Further, the hard copy it generates is unusable, since about every
eighth word is missing.  Also missing are a significant fraction of
spaces between words.

When you price the Alpha, be sure to include the cost of a full
documentation set and the bookshelf space needed to store it.  Do
not plan to rely on the online documentation.  It is unusable.

(DEC's official response was to use print screen to print a window
of documentation at a time.  Since dxbook crashes so frequently,
it can take quite a while to print any reasonable amount of
documentation.)                     

