rthomas@port.island.net (Robb Thomas) writes:

>If you can, avoid using file systems that do not sort their freelist.
>I know that a System V file system falls into this catagory.  The
>UFS file system may also be one of these very braindead file systems
>as well.  (does anyone know)?

The OSF/1 UFS FS (really the Berkeley FFS) uses a bitmap freelist and
is thus always sorted (for a suitable definition of `sorted', of
course.)  I've mounted OSF/1 UFS disks on little-endian 4.4BSD machines
and it works fine - even the partition labels match fine.

>[...] A number of Unix file systems are very braindead.  So
>braindead, that they don't bother to sort their FreeList during
>usage.  [...]

These would generally be USG derived unixen.  They also tend to have
the 65k inode limit, which is a killer for partitions larger than
about 140 MB in a news spool context.  The USG filesytem is very dated
and should be avoided at any cost.

>[...]

>Rumor: Split NEWS SPOOL vs rest of the system to seperate spindles.

Hardly a rumour.  Multiple spindles gives you an opportunity to
have multiple outstanding I/Os and reduces the likelyhood of needing
to seek on a given spindle (especially in the journaling areas like
/usr/local/news and /var/log/news).  Hot areas (under INN):

	/usr/local/news
	/var/log/news
	/var/spool/news/out.going	(if you have downstream sites)
	/var/spool/news/over.view	(if/how you're running overchan)

plus the obvious traffic in the /var/spool/news/foo directories.  I've
got this stuff spread across three spindles (am planning to go to
five) and an MFS; the load's balanced to about 20%.  Giving alt.* it's
own disk is a good first step.

(FWIW, I usually build my news filesystems as "-b 4096 -f 512 -i 2048
-a 8 -d 2" under SunOS or BSD.  Under DEC OSF/1, you're kind of stuck with
-b 8192 -f 1024, which is a pretty poor default for news (and a bogus
restriction.)  The small block/frag values hold down internal
fragmentation, and the small inode allocation quantum avoids inode
starvation at the expense of burst transfer speeds.)

regards,
Ross



	

