I posted this message a few days ago, but I'm reposting it because I'm not
certain that our news server was propogating articles off-site at the time.

I've been given the fun task of tracking down a problem we've been having
with our LAT-based connections to our main OSF/1 box ever since we upgraded
from  3.0 to 3.2.  This affects all of our OSF/1 3.2 systems that have LAT
enabled to allow login connections from our terminal servers.

Simply put, when a user logs in via a /dev/lat/* device, they are unable to
use stty to change some of the settings of their terminal.  Here's what
will come up:

    grace/glp5941[31]% stty
    #2 disc;speed 0 baud; -parity clocal
    erase = ^?
    brkint -inpck -istrip icrnl -ixany onlcr
    echo echoe echok
    grace/glp5941[32]% stty 9600
    grace/glp5941[33]% stty -clocal
    grace/glp5941[35]% stty istrip
    grace/glp5941[36]% stty
    #2 disc;speed 0 baud; -parity clocal
    erase = ^?
    brkint -inpck icrnl -ixany onlcr
    echo echoe echok

'stty istrip' will enable this mode.  However, attempting to set the line
speed had no effect, nor did changing the 'clocal' mode.  This seems to
work normally with any normal /dev/tty* device.

While this may seem trivial, we have several products -- most notably vi --
that do not respond well to thinking that the line speed is 0 bps.  Some
software will behave different when it believes the line speed is below a
certain value, presumably to make it easier for people with really old,
slow modems to deal with a system that would otherwise be slogging down
their terminals with screen redraws and large bursts of text that the modem
(or the user) wouldn't be able to keep up with.

Our technical support people are unable to explain this.  In fact, they
asked me to do research on this. :)  Has anyone got a clue what is causing
this, and how we could get around it?

-- 
