In article <43nnfh$ri0@nntp5.u.washington.edu>,
Donn Cave <donn@u.washington.edu> wrote:
>May also be a network service that isn't closing properly, in which case
>the kernel fix will only reduce the number of times per week.  With us, it
>was ftpd.  Run ``netstat -an'' some time after you have been running a day
>or two, see if you have many connections in some moribund state.  If it's
>ftpd, the connections will involve ports 23 and 21.

Port 23 is telnet; you want to look for 20 and 21.

>The ftpd bug affects most servers, not just OSF/1's;  DEC could fix it but
>hasn't seen fit to yet.

If a connection between two systems is broken for some reason, and your
system has some data queued up to go to the other system, it's going to
stay there until your system can contact the other system.  That's why TCP
is "reliable" - it's going to try to get that data delivered until
something says give up. 

This happens on most of the systems we run here (Digital Unix, Ultrix,
SunOS, Solaris). 

