Philippe Canal (pcanal@fndaub.fnal.gov) wrote:

: With the following:
: OSF1 V3.2 214 alpha
: cc  (cc)
:         The DEC OSF/1 AXP Compiler Driver 3.11
: I compile the following program:

:      char       array[2101250]; 

: I was wondering if it was a bug or a feature of OSF/1, its cc or the alpha,
: and is there any patch/promise of solving it in the next version etc ...

The default stack size for your machine is probably set absymally low.
In a C shell, type 
limit

On my machine, it responds with
cputime         unlimited
filesize        unlimited
datasize        1048576 kbytes
stacksize       32768 kbytes
coredumpsize    unlimited
memoryuse       121808 kbytes
descriptors     4096 files
addressspace    1048576 kbytes

giving me 32 Mb stack. You can try
limit stacksize unlimited
to get a bigger stack. If someone has set maxssiz in the sys/conf/<machinename>
file to a real low value & made a new kernel, however, there's nothing you can 
do except change the file & recompile the kernel.

I always set the dfldsiz & dflssiz  to the maxdsiz & maxssiz (respectively)
on my Alphas. Of course, I never share my machines, either ;^)

