From: di04@hrzpub.th-darmstadt.de (A.Schindler)
Subject: Re: telnet session remains after abnormal logout
Date: 1995/09/05
Message-ID: <42ij5l$m6e@rs18.hrz.th-darmstadt.de>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 109533864
references: <42ep2h$7lt@temasek.teleview.com.sg>
organization: Technische Hochschule Darmstadt
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

ccchua@temasek.teleview.com.sg wrote:
: Why doesn't a telnet session on a Digital Unix Ver 3.0 host terminate when 
: I logout abnormally?

Is this a bug, or is it a feature ?

As far as I knwow, modern Telnet flavours do no longer send the annoying
BSD 4.? keep alive packets. So, what should make Your server recon the 
break of the connection if simply no  packets arrive ? Has the user
fallen asleep ? If the server is running an application which *uses* the
link (say: tries to send packets out) then sooner (or later) an error
should show up due to "too many retries".

The situation is completely different if You use a server-homed serial PPP
connection or a really smart terminal server. The device/driver which 
has the facilities to detect the line break is responsible to take the 
(unix dialect) "interface" down and NOW all further incoming packets
should be answered by an ICMP "Host/Net unreachable message" (may take
a while too, until Your server will honour this).

Consider a *really* slow WAN link. It may take up to several minutes from
the arrival of one packet to the next. I don't know any Telnet that 
kills the link due to silence (no traffic) by default.

A.G.Schindler (Please reply mail to schindler@iid.com)





