ben@sys.toronto.edu (Benjamin Gamsa) wrote:
>
>My understanding is that the two numbers quoted (200ns and 265ns) are
>for the base 8000 platform and the 8200/8400 systems respectively.  The
>base platform can run the system clock up to 100MHz, but the 8200/8400
>systems use a 4x clock divider for the 300MHz Alpha, resulting in a
>system clock of 75MHz.  This, plus presumably some extra interface
>logic for the Alphas (I've only scanned one of the articles) would
>likely account for the latency differences.
>
You have to read the article carefully (I have not, but did talk to the 
authors), and seprate the goals and capabilities of the platform versus 
what the current shipping product achieves. The platform is designed to 
work with the bus at 10 ns, but that will not happen until there is a 400 
MHz processor. The current 300 MHz processors run the bus at 13.33 ns. 
The memory latency is 20 cycles or 200 ns (100 MHz bus) or 267 ns (75 MHz 
bus). The 265 ns is probably an editorial round off.

Dileep




