The patches on ftp.service.digital.com are fed from an internal system that
also feeds patches for DSNlink and DIA.  These patches have been approved,
tested and certified via an internal process call STD204.  STD204 defines a
process so that all kits for a given platform look similar and install in a
consistant manner.  Most all engineering groups submit thier patches via this
system and they are therefore available via FTP, DSNlink and DIA.

However, there are some groups that have in the past declined to follow the
processes set up (among them Ultrix Engineering and Digital Unix Engineering)
and their patches are not available via these 3 mediums.  Some of the patches
that are present for Digital Unix and Ultrix are there because they are 
security patches that were correctly submitted.

By calling the Customer Support Center (CSC) at (800)354-9000, supported
customers can obtain the patches that are not available via the above 3
mediums.  Those at the CSC have been trying for the past several years to
put pressure on the engineering groups that do not follow the STD204 process
to comply, but management support in the Engineering organizations for these
groups has been non-existent.

As Digital employees our voices don't carry enough weight to help rectify this
situation, so those of us that manage the Internet Patch Archives here at 
Digital have setup an alias that you can send your comments to and they will
be forwarded onto the persons that can show customer opinion, the e-mail
address for this purpose is patch-feedback@service.digital.com.

As for the search engine, the README files for the patches present are
searchable.  If it didn't make it into the archive, it didn't make it into the
searchable database.

Also please note that due to Government restrictions, not all patches are
available via anonymous-ftp.  Those patches that are not available via
anonymous-ftp need to be obtained from the Customer Support Center.

Digital's Commercial Internet Gateway Management




