I am using the rounding mode instructions as part of the Alpha 
architecture  (e.g. addtm, addtd, multm, multd, ....).

At first (and maybe still) I thought DEC was clever in introducing these 
seperate instructions in order to do directed rounding, rather than call 
a seperate instruction to set a control register before ordinary "add", 
"mult", etc. instructions.  However, now I'm wondering what the cost is.

All architectures I have worked on previously used the seperate 
instruction (to change a value in the rounding control register) method 
for permitting IEEE recommend (or is it required now) directed rounding.  
The timings for the interval arithmetic software I was using seemed 
reasonable (on an Ultrix box, for instance).

On an Alpha, I'm finding out that a simple interval arithmetic program which 
runs in 20 seconds on a DEC 5000/240 takes over 3 minutes to run on a DEC 
3000/400 with 64 M.  I have subsequently done profiling to try to get a 
handle on this problem.

The profiler tells me that the alpha program only took 10 seconds.  Using 
"time" I verfied that, sure enough, the user time was about 10 seconds, 
but the system time was around 3 minutes!

Basically I'm wondering what I may be doing wrong.  The inline assembly 
seems pretty straight forward, and I even seperated out the assembly 
instructions into a seperate file, rather than inlining.  It seems from a 
profile, that the timings are reasonable.  However, the enourmous system 
time (about 25 times the cost of the user computation time) is 
distressing.  Any ideas?

Please send me email about this.  I don't have convenient or regular 
access to news.



