In article <41ta3d$ram@lectura.CS.Arizona.EDU>, scott@CS.Arizona.EDU (Scott E Gilbert) writes:
> 
> On a similar note is anyone else having trouble getting DNS to work
> correctly with just subdomain names?  I have no problem when I specify the
> full path, or a short name in my own subdomain, but specifying another
> subdomain at arizona.edu doesn't seem to work.  To make this clear:
> 
>   name.subdomain.domain.edu   works
>   name                        works when in the same sub domain
>   
>     but
>   
>   name.subdomain              fails even when in the same sub domain.
> 
> For reference, I've seen this problem on two AlphaStation 200 4/100 with
> version 3.2B of OSF/1 (oops I mean DEC Unix :).  I've checked with other
> system administartors and my /etc/resolv.conf and /etc/hosts look correct.
> 

I don't know about 3.2B, but my release notes for 3.2 specifically mention
this.  Section 4.3.1.2 of version 3.2 release notes specifies that the
resolver's search list contains ONLY the default domain.  Previous versions
contained parent domains.  The release notes claim that this is in keeping
with RFC 1535.  I haven't read this RFC, so I have to assume that this is
accurate.

You can work around this with a change in resolv.conf.  Instead of having
a "domain" directive, use the "search" directive.  If your old domain
line looked like:

	domain a.b.c.d.e

your search line might look like

	search a.b.c.d.e b.c.d.e c.d.e d.e

which would mimic the old behavior.  Note that if both a search and a
domain directive are placed in resolv.conf, (or multiples of either, for
that matter) then only the LAST one has any effect.
